WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.300
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach. So today we are going

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:12.560
to go through some examples of FSM's and I'm going to present a couple of them in these

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.560
mini lectures and then we are going to work on some in class. So here is the example why.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:30.560
We are asked to draw a state transition diagram of a Moore FSM state machine that has one

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:42.280
input, A, one output out, and two states, LO, which stands for low, and HI, which stands

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.280
for high.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:52.080
The FSM operation is such that if input is equal to zero, we need to either go to or

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.360
or stay in state low.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.720
If input is equal to one, we have to go to

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.640
or stay in a state high.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.980
Since this is more FSM machine

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.920
where the output depends on the state,

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.280
we are going to define that with state low,

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.040
output is going, output out is equal to zero.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.040
And for state high, output out is equal to one.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:29.060
to 1. Now let's look at the the machine itself. So what did it say? How many states do we have?

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:44.680
This problem is very well defined, most of it. So we all know by the description of the problem

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:51.800
that we have two states. What we don't know is what is the starting state. And we can decide,

00:01:51.800 --> 00:02:11.120
make a design decision. Low will be starting state. So you're going to make

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:17.840
two nice round circles. I probably could have made them even larger and one is

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.360
going to be low and other one is going to be high. And we made design decision

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:28.640
that low is starting state so that means that we have this edge that is coming

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:37.840
from quote-unquote nowhere and coming to state low and that is annotating that low is the starting

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:47.680
state. So because of the description of problem it says if a is equal to zero we are going to

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:56.560
stay or go to low. Well okay as I mentioned previously we have one variable that variable

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:07.120
has two potential states. We have to have two outgoing edges for low. One when a is equal to zero

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:17.600
and one is when a is equal to one. So okay when a is equal to zero according to a equals to zero

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:25.520
if we are in low we are going to stay in low state and we can say on a prime

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:33.520
alternatively we can write there on zero. Now what is going to happen if we are in state low

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:46.960
and we have to the output that is equal to one if we get output that is one go to state

00:03:50.260 --> 00:04:00.530
so let's say we do it this way and we check our state low which is here and see that we have two

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:06.870
outgoing edges from that state. So we are covering all possible transitions from

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:17.350
the state low for all possible combinations of input A. Now we have to

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:24.910
do a second part and I'm just kind of going to change the color so that we are

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:37.330
a little bit more obvious, so if we are in state high and we get value a, we are going

00:04:37.330 --> 00:05:01.550
to stay in high. If we are in state high and we get input zero, we go to state low. So

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:06.550
That is pretty much it, except we forgot one thing.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.950
We didn't define outputs.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:14.950
And outputs, because this is a more FSM,

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:21.670
they're going to depend on the state itself.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:26.670
So for state low, output is going to be over zero.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:30.110
For state high, output is going to be one.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:38.310
Let's kind of play a little bit of game and see what happens if we start our FSM.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:39.850
And I'm just going to narrate this.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:48.670
If we start FSM, we are going to go into stay low and our output is going to be low.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:56.040
Now if next thing we get zero, we're going to stay in this same state.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.160
The next input is also again zero.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.600
We are going to stay in that state again.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.040
However, once we get one,

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.080
we are going to just transition to state high

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.500
and we are going to start the output one.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.520
If the following state is,

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.760
sorry, if the following input is one,

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:20.040
we are going to stay in high and keep output one.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.240
If the next state is one,

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.720
we're going to stay here and keep output one.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:30.380
If the next input is 1, stay in high and keep out at 4.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.140
First time when we encounter

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.460
input 0, we're going to transition to low and produce output 0.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:44.100
If we have input 0,

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:49.580
we're going to stay in low and we are going to generate output 0.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.500
And that happens forever and ever.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:58.900
Mind you, a couple of things that I'm going to kind of go and with one of them

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:03.860
I'm going to remind you from what we did in many lectures, the other one I'm

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:14.260
going to jump forward. So first time, very often, FSM's are defined such that

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:20.060
only on positive edge of the clock we consider what is the value of input. So

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:27.340
that means that we are going to have all sorts of transitions that are going to be between one

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:35.100
positive edge of clock and other positive edge of clock. Even when I say, hey, let's say we start

00:07:35.100 --> 00:08:00.120
with state, we are in state low, and on next, positive edge, for example, A is equal to

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:11.120
zero. What happens? Well, we are going to stay in that state low. And only in the following

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:26.590
edge. If A changes, if A becomes 1, and it did in our example, we will go to state high.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:40.290
Now, I'm going to show the output here, and this output is going to depend on the state.

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:45.290
So here I'll put a zero, here I'll put a zero,

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:53.940
this moment out becomes one.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.100
The other thing that I said that I'm going to mention

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.040
with kind of jumping forward,

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:00.240
because only next class we are going to do something

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.500
that is called state encoding.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.880
So we have this state that is called low

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:06.400
and state that is fall high.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:09.060
We have to represent it somehow in binary form.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:18.040
And that's what is called state encoding.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:20.120
This is just a side note.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:25.120
Sometimes state encoding can be equal to the output

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:31.420
that the state is producing.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:40.540
So sometimes we have this equivalent

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:43.460
and then we basically do not need to generate

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:45.940
yet another value for the output.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:47.560
So that does happen.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:48.860
Just keep that in mind.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:51.740
Next example in the next video.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:52.740
Thanks for watching.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:55.380
Thanks for watching!

